   100	
   101	He had not decided anything. That was the thing Cael understood in the dark, without a word from anyone: that a man who carries a thing for four years without opening it is not a man who has decided to keep it. He is a man who has decided not to decide, every day, and has found that the not-deciding is a thing you can carry, and has carried it, and has walked it now into the one place on the road where the decision had a counter to be made at. Seln had gone up the hill to look at the counter. Not to use it. To look. As he had looked at a landing door for a winter, and at four crossbows in scrub, and at a sheet with a bar of ink on it: to know where a thing was, exactly, so that he could keep on not going to it on purpose instead of by accident.
