   117	Vastin, that week, was learning to be nobody, and was bad at it.
   118	
   119	Cael catalogued the badness because it was honest and because he had never once seen a man try. The man carried the kit's case down the stair each morning on the knee and set it down against the rock and stood beside it, and did not draft anything, and did not offer anything, and did not say his name to anyone, and the not-doing sat on him like a coat cut for a bigger man. He had stood at the head of things for forty years, and standing at the foot of them was a thing his body did not know the shape of: he stood too straight for a hand; he watched the readers like an officer at a briefing and not like labour watching a woman work; and when Cleon turned his head for the lamp, Vastin's hand moved before Cael's did, every time, and stopped, because the lamp was Cael's, and the stopping was the whole of his week.
