     1	# Chapter 12 — No Authority
     2	
     3	The alley had steps in it, and that was all the geography, and he had it before the first man moved because he had walked the alley eleven times with a box on his shoulder and a cataloguer walks a thing eleven times so that he has it on the twelfth.
     4	
     5	The first tier's stair came down off the bluff to a landing at the first wall's foot, and from the landing the route to the ring road was not the wide street the carts used but a cut between two rows of houses that Seln had found on the second day and used since, because a working party that went home by the carts' street was a working party that could be counted going home. The cut was stepped — twenty-two steps, in five flights, each flight a stride and a half wide and walled on both sides by the backs of houses with no doors in them, three men high, sheer — and it fell from the landing to the inn's yard gate in a straight line that a man at the top could see the bottom of, and a man at the bottom could see the top of, and nobody in between could see anything but wall. A choke. He had thought that on the second day, and had said it to Seln, and Seln had said, "Yes. That's why. Nobody holds a choke they can't get out of, so nobody holds this one," and had been right for eight days.
     6	
     7	On the two hundred and ninetieth day, at dusk, with the boxes, coming down the third flight, he saw that somebody had decided to hold it after all.
     8	
     9	Four at the top. He counted them as he had counted hounds and hoods — without seeing one clearly — as they came out of the landing's shadow onto the first flight above and behind the party: four figures, not carters, not in a colour, with their hoods up in a city where nobody wore a hood at dusk in spring, and one of them with a thing at his shoulder that was a crossbow, in a city, which was a thing nobody carried in a city unless they had brought it for a purpose. And two at the bottom, coming through the inn's yard gate from the ring road, which they had gone round by, with staves, into the fifth flight, so that the cut had a lid on it and a floor, and the party — Karis, and four hands with four boxes, and the cache on a man who was not there — was in the middle three flights, on steps, with wall on either side, and no room to do anything a Path did except be loud.
    10	
    11	He catalogued it because a fight is drawn before it is fought. Six of them. A crossbow above. Staves below. Twenty-two steps, sheer walls, dusk. Karis on the third flight with the flat. Brom above her with the first box on the good shoulder and the bound one carried. Lira on the second flight with the third box on two arms. Vastin on the fourth flight, below them all, with the kit's case and the stick, on the knee, nearest the two coming up. And Cael on the third flight beside Karis with the middle box and the satchel under it, ten things in him and one of them a burst that would have been the loudest thing in the cut and would have brought a bolt down it from the top in the beat after, which was, he understood, what the crossbow was for: they had come for practitioners. They had come for a crew that walked out of quiet ground declaring, and they had built the trap for the declaration, and the trap was a stone cut with a bolt at the top of it.
    12	
    13	Seln was not anywhere. Good.
    14	
    15	"Boxes down," Cael said. Not loud. "Karis, against the wall. Nobody declares."
