    22	
    23	He rooted the choke above them.
    24	
    25	Cael had seen it done once, on a road, across a cut with ice in it, and had learned then what a wall was and what it cost, and had thought, since, that he knew all of it. He had not. On a road a wall was a plane across a line with nothing on either side of it but scree, and a pack had gone round it on the loose slope, slow, and the man holding it had been the loudest thing in a country with nothing behind him but a man with a rock. This was a cut. This was a stride and a half of stone between two walls three men high with no door in either, and Vastin turned on the fourth flight — turned uphill, with his back to the two coming up from the yard gate and his face to the four coming down from the landing, which was the wrong way round for a man defending himself and the only way round for a man defending a party — and went past Lira and Brom and Karis and Cael on the third flight, on the knee, with the stick, faster than a man with that knee had any business going up steps, and stopped on the second flight below the four, and set his feet, and the plane went up.
    26	
    27	Cael went up after him. Two steps, from the third flight to the second's top, with the box left where it lay and the knife out, because the plane's foot was where the reason for the plane should stand, and he stood there, two feet below it, and did not go further, because further was the plane.
    28	
    29	Across the cut. Wall to wall, because the cut's walls were a stride and a half apart and a plane knows what it was told, and the walls were sheer and the plane went from one to the other without a hair of a gap, and from the second flight's top step to somewhere above a man's head, and it was, Cael saw — from two feet below it, looking up — the first thing he had ever watched Iron Wall do that the ground did not argue with. On a road the ground had said *go round*. Here the ground said *there is no round*. Three of the four at the top came down the first flight at a run and hit the plane as eight hounds had hit it, and did not go over each other, because they were men and men do not; they went into a thing that was not there and stopped as if it were the cut's own stone, and the crossbow, which had been coming up to a shoulder, went off into the plane a hand from the man's face, and the bolt did what the ice's bolts had done on a floor, which was nothing, and fell.
