    49	Brom came down off the third flight to meet the first stave, and did not go hard, because a healer had said not a door, not a box, not a man, and because he had learned at a ford that Iron Skin was a shout; he came down as a very large man with a rock in his good hand, which he had picked up off the fourth step where the cut's makers had left a loose one a long time ago, and put his shoulder into the stave's man as a man puts a shoulder into a cart that is on its side, which was a thing he had done once this week already, and the stave's man went into the wall, and the stave went down the steps, and Brom stood on it. He did not hit the man again. He had not needed to. He stood on the stave with his bound shoulder carried still and forward and his good hand with the rock in it and looked at the second man coming up, and the second man, who had been told to expect a practitioner, looked at a big man with a rock standing on his friend's stave, and did not know what to hit.
