    64	
    65	Cael did not see it. He saw the result, which was the thing you saw with Seln, and drew the rest afterward, because a fight is drawn after as well as before.
    66	
    67	Three of the four above the plane had come down onto the first flight. The fourth had not. He was on the landing at the cut's head, in the shadow where the stair came down, standing where a man stands who is not going to fight and is going to tell the others when to — and Cael had counted six and had had six, four and two, and had been right about the number and wrong about what one of them was for, because the one you do not count until he speaks was the one he had counted as a fighter. He spoke. He said one word up the cut, from the landing, to the crossbow, a word Cael did not catch and did not need to, because a word said from the back of a fight to the front of it is a signal, and a signal is a coordination, and a coordination is a thing you can remove.
    68	
    69	Seln removed it.
    70	
    71	He was on the roof of the house on the cut's east side — he had been there, Cael worked out later, since the party had come down the first flight, because Seln went home by a roof when a cut had two ends and he could only watch one — and the landing was under the roof's edge, and the sixth man was on the landing, and a man on a landing in a city at dusk does not look up, because nobody in a city looks up. A cord. That was all it was. A weighted cord, from above, round the sixth man's arm and the rail he stood beside, so that the arm he signalled with was tied to the stair, and then Seln was not on the roof any more and the sixth man was pulling at his own arm in the shadow and saying nothing, because a man tied to a rail does not say the next word; he works at the cord. Nobody died. Nobody was cut. The signal stopped.
