   103	And Vastin.
   104	
   105	Cael did not have a catalogue for it, and had not had one on a road either, and had one now, because he had sat beside it while the man's breath came back.
   106	
   107	He had stood two feet from it. That was the fact. He had stood on the second flight's last step, at the plane's foot, for all of it — a minute; he had counted it, because he counted things, and it had been a minute and some — with a crossbow above and his own body the reason the crossbow wanted through, and had watched, from two feet, a Silver-tier Iron Wall held across a corridor by a man who could not move and would not stop, and the plane had been *there*, the whole minute, at the edge of his eye, a place where the air had been told it was stone and had believed it, and the believing had been the man, and the man had been the plane's foot, and Cael had stood beside the foot and been the reason the plane was needed, for a minute, under real threat, with nothing in him declared.
   108	
   109	And he had it.
   110	
   111	He did not know when. Somewhere in the minute. Not at the start, when the four hit the plane; not at the end, when it came off; somewhere in the middle, in the long part where nothing happened and the plane held and the four stood at it and the crossbow was spent and he stood still, the thing had gone into him the way the ten had gone in — not taken; *learned*, at two feet, by a body that learned the shape of a thing by standing beside it long enough — and he had felt it go and had not looked at it, because he was being the reason and the reason does not look away. He looked now. On the step, beside a man who could not move, with the costs catalogued and the cut empty, he put his attention where the thing was, the way he had been taught to on a table, and it rendered.
   112	
   113	*Iron-Wall-adjacent. Vastin. Partial. Iron family. Held. A plane, not a body: the ground told not to give. Distinct from the third — Iron-adjacent, the bed; this is not the bed hardened, this is a line drawn on it and kept. Engagement field: defensive; rooted. Tier equivalent unknown. Deployment: none. Eleventh.*
   114	
   115	He read it once and let it stand and did not use it. There was nothing to use it on; the cut was empty. He sat with it. The man beside him breathed.
   116	
   117	"You held it," Cael said.
   118	
   119	"I held it." Vastin's voice was the ledger's, with nothing left in the ledger. "There was nothing behind it. I have never once in my life held a line with nothing behind it. There's always been — " He stopped. "A field. A formation. The Compact. I held it because I decided to, and that was all that was behind it, and it held."
   120	
   121	"It held."
   122	
   123	"It nearly didn't. I could feel the place where it wouldn't have." He looked at his own hands, on his knees, on a step. "I have put that plane in front of a great many people in forty years. I have never once put it in front of anyone the Compact would have restricted." He did not look at Cael. "I know what you are, on the paper. I have read the paper more times than any man alive. I put the wall in front of it anyway. I'd like that entered somewhere, since I'm not the one who keeps the ledger any more."
   124	
   125	Cael did not say anything for a while. Then he said, "It's entered," and took the log out, on the step, and wrote it, and did not tell the man what else he had entered, because a fragment is a thing you own and a thing a man gave you is a thing you owe, and he did not yet have a word for the distance between those two, and was not going to make one up on a step.
   126	
   127	---
   128	
   129	*Two hundred and ninetieth day. Treswick, the cut between the first-tier stair and the inn's yard. Six of them, faceless, hooded, a crossbow and staves; one of the six on the landing to say when. They came for a declaration. None taken. Nobody dead. The cache kept — it was on a roof. Karis, left hand, a cut, bound with thread and a shirt. Brom, the shoulder carried; the good one used wrong. Lira, nothing, two arms. Seln, nothing; a cord on a rail. Vastin, spent past a road; the knee again. Me, the burst not used, on purpose; nothing declared; a minute at a plane's foot, being the reason. A hundred and thirty-six in hand.*
   130	
   131	*Eleventh. Iron Wall, from Vastin, in an alley, while he held a line for us with nothing behind him but the decision to. It's not Brom's — Brom gets harder, this holds. I've spent five years learning to give ground and disappear. Now I've got the opposite in me, from the man who spent forty years being the wall they put in front of people like me. He held it for us instead. I don't have a word for what that is. I wrote it down anyway.*

