     5	Rock first. The quarry's own rock, cut in the days before anyone cut rock square, in galleries that went off from the landing in three directions at a height a man could walk in and a width two men could pass in, and the cut-marks still on every face of it, the marks of tools nobody made any more, running as the quarrymen had run them and not as a mason would. Then the shelving, which was not shelving as the Compact shelved a thing — no cases, no doors, no labels — but timber pinned into the rock floor to roof along both walls of every gallery, grey with age and dry as bone, and on the timber, the records.
     6	
     7	He catalogued them by their shapes because he could not catalogue them by anything else. Rolls, the size of a man's forearm, tied, with the ties gone to dust and the rolls staying rolled by habit. Boards — flat pieces of wood the length of a hand with a face to them, a hundred to a shelf, standing on their edges like slates. Bound leaves, thick, sewn along one side with something that had been cord, in covers of a leather that had been a colour once. Things that were none of those: a sheet of hammered metal with marks on it, standing against the rock; a stone, squared, with a face cut into it; a bundle of sticks tied in a pattern that was, he understood after a while, the pattern itself, and not a bundle. Centuries of a country's paper, if it was paper, in scripts that were not the Compact's and had not been anyone's for longer than the Compact had had a name, kept because a decision had never been made about them, in a hole, in the dark, under a building whose whole purpose was to decide.
     8	
     9	And the smell. He had no catalogue for it. It was the smell of a thing that has been left alone for so long that the leaving-alone has become the thing, and he stood on the landing with a box on his shoulder and breathed it and understood that he had never once, in nineteen years, stood in a place where nothing had been decided, and that this was what it smelled like.
    10	
    11	The lamp went on into the middle gallery, and there was a light at the end of it that had gone ahead of them.
    12	
    13	---
    14	
    15	Cleon was already reading.
    16	
    17	That was the truest thing about the man, and the first: they came down the middle gallery with the lamp and the boxes, five people and the sound of five people in a place that had not heard five people in longer than the walls had stood, and at the gallery's end, where the rock opened into a chamber that the quarrymen had left when they hit a seam they did not want, there was a table, and a lamp on it that was always lit, and a man at the table with a bound thing open in front of him and his finger on a line, reading, who did not look up.
    18	
    19	He had gone ahead down the middle gallery with his own lamp while the hands got the boxes off the landing, and had been at the table long enough to find his line before they reached it. He had a chair with a cushion on it that had been a cushion for thirty years, and a second lamp on a bracket in the rock above the table that a hand had put there to throw light where a reader wanted it, and behind the table, along the chamber's back wall, shelving that was not the archive's — newer timber, squared, with a man's own order on it: bound leaves in one hand, a hand Cael knew from a ledger in a coat, hundreds of them, thirty years of a man writing down what he had read in a room nobody else read in, and the room's own things pulled from the galleries and stood on the new shelves where he could reach them, in an order that was his and would die with him.
    20	
    21	He read to the end of the line. Then to the end of the page. Then he put a scrap of leather in it and closed the thing and put both hands flat on it, which was, Cael saw, exactly what Karis did, and looked up, and looked at Karis, and at nothing else in the chamber.
    22	
    23	"Put them there," he said, and moved the bound thing off the table's middle.
    24	
    25	Karis put them there.
    26	
    27	She did it as she had put a ring on a table and a wall on a floor and a marshal's six years on a slab: without saying she was going to, and all at once, and in the order that was in her head. The oilcloth opened. Fifty-two sheets, in three sets, laid out along the table's length in the order of the floors — the Ring at the left end, eleven; the Stair in the middle, twenty-nine; the Hall at the right, twelve — with the lamp moved once to put the light where the sets met, and the charcoal on the sheets grey against the paper as it had been grey on a floor in the dark, and she did not say a word while she laid them, and neither did he, and the four hands stood with the boxes against the chamber's wall and watched a man look at fifty-two sheets that he had, Cael thought, been looking for in some part of himself since before any of the four had been born.
    28	
    29	He did not touch them. He looked. He looked at the first set for as long as it takes a man to read a page he already knows, and then at the second, longer, and then at the third, and his hand came up off the table and went toward the third set and stopped, a hand's width off, and stayed there, and Cael saw it stay and had seen a hand do that before, on a table, near a page.
    30	
    31	"Three floors," Cleon said.
    32	
    33	"Three floors. Two hundred metres each, to the line. A fourth we didn't go in."
    34	
    35	"A fourth." His hand had not moved. "And the word."
    36	
    37	Karis said it. "Older than [UNBOUND]," she said, as she had said it at a table in the autumn, once, as a stratum's limit and not as a meaning — the old word, the retired one, the one from the confiscated record that she had carried across two seasons and a Line and had put in a sentence exactly once — and Cleon closed his eyes.
    38	
    39	Not for long. For the length of a man deciding whether a thing he has hoped for and dreaded in equal measure for thirty years is the one or the other, and finding that it is both, and that both is not what the young man who started the work had imagined it would be. He opened them. He looked at the third set, at the Hall's twelve sheets, at a mark on the fourth of them that Cael could not have told from any other mark, and said, quietly, to the sheet and not to Karis:
    40	
    41	"I know that hand." He did not say from where. "You've brought it in off the ground. Do you know what that is, to a man who's been sitting in a hole for thirty years being tolerated?"
    42	
    43	"I know what a marsh with a kit's ledger in it was to me," Karis said. "I'd guess it's that, longer."
    44	
    45	"It's that, longer." He put his hand down flat on the table beside the third set, not on it. "I'll tell you the thing I tell nobody, because you've earned it by walking in, and because you'll need to know it before you sit down in this chair, which you're going to. Being right for thirty years alone is a thing a man can carry. Being believed — " He stopped. "I had thought it would be a relief. It's not. It's the end of being able to be wrong. I've been wrong, safely, in this room, for thirty years. Nobody read the work, so the work could be anything. Now it's going to be read, and it's going to be *this*" — the hand a finger's width above the sheets — "and if I've been right, then what I've been right about is not a thing a man is glad to be right about." He looked at her for a moment longer. "Give me a week. Give me a week with these and the text and I'll tell you what you've got. And then you'll not sleep either, and I'm sorry for it, and I'm not going to stop."
