    58	
    59	Brom, beside him, had set his box down and had sat on it, with the shoulder still and forward and the foot out, and was watching Karis work with the face he kept for somebody else pricing a wall: respect, and no intention of touching it. Lira was on her feet, with her two hands at her sides, and was not watching Karis; she was watching the galleries going off into the dark in three directions, the way she watched a floor for the place where a thing would come from, because a hole was a hole. And Vastin had set the kit's case down and stood beside it on the knee, with the stick, and was looking at the shelving behind Cleon's chair — the thirty years of one man's hand — with an expression Cael had not seen on him and had, after a while, a name for: it was the look of a man who has spent a lifetime in offices reading what other men filed, and has just understood that this man had filed the whole of his life in a hole where no one would ever read it, and had done it anyway, and had been right.
    60	
