    94	
    95	He stood with it. He stood in the lane with the kit's case on his shoulder and the stick in his hand and his face doing the thing a face does when a man has just found out that the reflex he is proudest of — the one that had ended every pause in his life, the fact that ended pauses — is not a tool any more but a blade with no handle, and that the only way he could help the four people in the lane with him was to be no one, and that he did not know how to be no one, and had not known that he did not know until a half-breath ago.
    96	
    97	"I'm sorry," he said. To the lane. In the ledger voice, which had never in Cael's hearing said that sentence before.
    98	
    99	"Don't be sorry," Seln said. "Be nobody. It's the only thing down here that's worth anything, and you can't buy it and you can't be given it. You have to stop being the other thing." He went back to the lane's mouth. "Down. She's waiting."
   100	
   101	---
   102	
   103	They went down. Cael carried the box. It was a long stair and the box was the middle one and he had carried it up and down twice now and knew its weight to the hair, and he carried it behind Brom and ahead of Lira with the satchel under it and the man with the stick behind Lira, going down slowly, on the knee, in silence, and at the iron door with the mark on it at the height of a man's eye he set the box down to open the door, because the hand nearest the door opened it, and that was him.
   104	
   105	He looked at the man behind him while he did it.
   106	
   107	Vastin was on the landing, with the case, looking at the iron. He was not looking at the mark. He was looking at the door as a thing that opened on a place where the name he had just spoken was worth nothing, not because the place had decided that, but because the place was older than the name, and he was, Cael thought, working out how a man walks into a place like that, and finding that he had never once in his life had to.
