   109	Cael had. He had walked into every room of his life as the thing the room could not use, and had learned, at fourteen, in a room with a door that locked from the outside, that being nothing to a room was a thing you could stand in if you stood in it on purpose. He had been no one for five years. He had been no one on purpose for nine months. He was, at that moment, on a landing under a city, the nearest thing on the continent to an expert in the exact thing a fifty-three-year-old man was standing behind him failing to do, and he did not say so, because a man who has learned a thing at a price does not hand it to the man who set the price for free, and because it was not his to hand; the man would learn it or he would not, and the learning was the man's.
