     9	He catalogued it because Cleon had let him hold it, once, to move it into the light, and a thing you have held is a thing you owe a description. A board. Not a leaf; a flat piece of a wood he did not know, the length of a forearm and a hand wide, gone the colour of old iron, with a face to it, and on the face, cut in with something finer than a chisel, lines. Not letters. He knew the Compact's letters and had seen four scripts that were not the Compact's on the shelves in three days, and these were none of the five, so far as he could tell: lines that went across the board's face in runs, some straight and some bent at the ends, with the bends the same at the ends of some runs and different at the ends of others, so that all the face looked, if you held it far enough from your eye, like a wall Karis had copied. He had thought that on the first day and had not said it. On the third day he did not need to, because that was the day Cleon put the board beside the Hall's fourth sheet and said, "Now."
