    55	Brom's offer came at the bell.
    56	
    57	Cael was in the yard for it, because the bell had sent the hands up and out of the cellar in the order they had gone down, and Brom had been first down and was first up, and Cael, with the middle box, came through the inn's yard gate a dozen strides behind him and stopped, because there was a cart on its side in the yard and a man of Brom's size standing at it with a box set down carefully at his feet.
    58	
    59	A broker's cart. It had come through the yard's gate too fast, by the evidence — a wheel in the drain, the bed over, the load shifted against the rails and held by the ropes — and the carter was at the beast's head with the traces fouled, and the broker's factor, a man in a coat that had a colour, stood at the cart's tail with a ledger under his arm and the look of a man pricing a loss.
    60	
    61	Brom set his shoulder to the cart's bed.
    62	
    63	He did it without a Path, because a healer had said not a door, not a box, not a man, and Brom kept a price; with the good shoulder and his back and his legs, and the foot with the tooth-hole in it taking the brace on the stones, and the bed came up, and hung, and the good shoulder said something Cael saw in the set of it and Brom did not, and the cart went back onto its wheels with the load on and stood there. Brom stepped back from it and put a hand on the box at his feet as if the box were the thing that wanted seeing to.
    64	
    65	The factor watched him do it. He watched him set the box down first and touch the cart second, and Cael saw him price it — the size of the man; the care; the Copper's paper that a man in that trade could see on a stranger as a smith sees a shoe — and the factor did not wait, because a broker who sees a thing worth having does not.
    66	
    67	"You've paper," he said. It was not a question.
    68	
    69	"Copper," Brom said. "Circuit-rated."
    70	
    71	"I've a yard here and a second on the second tier, and I've no one in either who could have done that without a Path, and two who couldn't have done it with one." He named the wage. It was more than the five had earned in the winter, and he said it as a man says a thing he knows is enough. "Guard's post. A pass — not the district's nothing; a working pass to the second tier with my name on it, and a bed inside the second wall. You'd be ranked, here. You'd be what you are."
    72	
    73	Brom listened to all of it. Cael, at the gate with the middle box, watched him listen — the shoulder still, the face he used for a wall somebody else was pricing — and knew from the face that Brom was pricing it honestly, because Brom priced everything honestly, and that it was a good price. It was the life. It was the one his family had wanted from him, at fourteen, with a Gold in their eye and a Copper in his hand; the one he had walked away from into a circuit and then into a road and then across a Line; the one that had been waiting, it turned out, in a broker's yard on the Line for a man big enough to put a cart back on its wheels. It was clean. Nobody at Lowmarch's plank had ever offered him anything clean.
    74	
    75	Brom picked up the box while the factor was still talking. He put it on the shoulder that was not the good one, because the good one had just paid for a cart and he knew it, and he would carry the bound one wrong for a day and say nothing, and Cael entered the debt where debts went.
    76	
    77	"I'll think on it," Brom said, which was courtesy, because Brom did not lie.
    78	
    79	Then he went back across the yard with the box on the wrong shoulder toward the inn's door and the morning and the stair and the hole under the city where a woman was reading a wall, and the factor watched him go with the face of a man who has priced a thing correctly and been declined by it, and did not understand, and did not need to.
    80	
    81	That was the whole statement. Cael wrote it as one, at the bell: *Brom was offered the life. He picked up the box.*
    82	
    83	He asked Cael one thing about it, later, on the stair, going down, with the boxes.
    84	
    85	"You think Karis is right? That it's all built on something?"
    86	
    87	"I think we're about to find out."
    88	
    89	Brom shifted the box. "Good. I'd hate to have turned down a real job for a wrong hunch."
    90	
    91	He was joking. Cael heard the joke. He also heard the other thing, under it, which was a man who had put a cart back on its wheels with his back because a healer had said not to use his shoulder, and had been offered a bed inside a wall for it, and had picked up a box, and wanted, very slightly, to be told that the box was going somewhere. Cael did not tell him. He did not know. He said, "So would I," which was true, and they went down.
    92	
    93	---
    94	
    95	Seln walked the tiers that night.
    96	
    97	Cael did not see him go and did not see him come back, and knew both, because a man who has slept in the same room as Seln for a year knows the shape of the room with Seln in it and the shape without, and woke at the second shape, some hour after the city's bell had gone for the last time, and lay in the dark and did not get up, because the thing Seln was doing was Seln's.
    98	
    99	He knew what it was. He had known since the ferry. The cache was on Seln's back — had been on Seln's back for four years, across a hearing and a Line and a rim with four crossbows in the scrub, carried and never opened and targeted once and kept — and it had walked, four days ago, into the first governed city Seln had stood in since he had walked out of one with it. And up the hill, on the crown, past three gates and a square, there was a registry, with a book in it and a counter and a careful young man with a pen, and behind the registry, somewhere in the west, the office the registry answered to, which would have paid for what was in the cache in a currency Seln had stopped taking, in a room he had stopped standing in, on a day he had stopped counting from.
   100	
   101	He had not decided anything. That was the thing Cael understood in the dark, without a word from anyone: that a man who carries a thing for four years without opening it is not a man who has decided to keep it. He is a man who has decided not to decide, every day, and has found that the not-deciding is a thing you can carry, and has carried it, and has walked it now into the one place on the road where the decision had a counter to be made at. Seln had gone up the hill to look at the counter. Not to use it. To look. As he had looked at a landing door for a winter, and at four crossbows in scrub, and at a sheet with a bar of ink on it: to know where a thing was, exactly, so that he could keep on not going to it on purpose instead of by accident.
   102	
   103	How he went up three tiers at night with no pass was not a thing Cael asked, then or after. He had a guess. The stairs cut in the bluff had rails, and the rails ended where the walls began, and the walls were old, and Seln had told a room once that a city's walls were built by people who wanted to keep things out and not by people who had ever tried to get in. He had a guess and he kept it, because a guess about Seln was a thing you kept.
   104	
   105	Seln came back before the light. Cael heard the room change shape and did not move, and Seln crossed to his blanket and lay down, and was still, and then said, to the dark, in the voice for an inventory, very low, so that only the one person in the room who was awake would have it:
   106	
   107	"I looked at it."
   108	
   109	That was all. Cael did not answer. He lay in the dark and had, from a man who said a thing once, the shape of the thing, which was as much as Seln ever gave, and the grit on a boot, which Cael had heard on the floor and not seen, said a roof: that Seln had gone up in the night — not to the crown; Seln did not stand beside a thing that read bodies; to somewhere under it, a roof in the district or the foot of a stair, with the cache on his back and the station's pale light above him — and had looked up at the door his old trade would have walked through, and had decided nothing, and had come back down, seen by nobody, and had let one person know that he had decided nothing, which was the nearest thing to trust that Seln had ever handed anyone, and was, Cael thought, a great deal.
   110	
   111	He wrote it down in the morning in six words, because six was what it was. *Seln looked at it. Decided nothing.*
   112	
   113	Lira, who had also been awake — she was always awake when the room changed shape, and had been since a ford — said nothing about it at all, then or after, which was the other half of the thing: a crew that keeps a man's not-deciding for him without once asking him to decide.
   114	
   115	---
   116	
   117	Vastin, that week, was learning to be nobody, and was bad at it.
   118	
   119	Cael catalogued the badness because it was honest and because he had never once seen a man try. The man carried the kit's case down the stair each morning on the knee and set it down against the rock and stood beside it, and did not draft anything, and did not offer anything, and did not say his name to anyone, and the not-doing sat on him like a coat cut for a bigger man. He had stood at the head of things for forty years, and standing at the foot of them was a thing his body did not know the shape of: he stood too straight for a hand; he watched the readers like an officer at a briefing and not like labour watching a woman work; and when Cleon turned his head for the lamp, Vastin's hand moved before Cael's did, every time, and stopped, because the lamp was Cael's, and the stopping was the whole of his week.
   120	
   121	On the fourth day he said, to Cael, at the rock wall, very quietly, "How long did it take you."
   122	
   123	"To be nobody?"
   124	
   125	"Yes."
   126	
   127	"I was fourteen. I'm nineteen." Cael moved the lamp. "I'm not there yet either."
   128	
   129	Vastin took that. He did not draft anything. He stood at the foot of a thing, badly, and kept standing.
   130	
   131	---
   132	
   133	Late on the fourth day, with the bell an hour off and the lamp low, Cleon put the Hall's fourth sheet beside the board for the last time that day and sat looking at the two of them for a long while without reading either, and then said, to Karis, in a voice Cael had not heard from him — not the voice for a limit; the other one, the one a man keeps for a thing he has carried alone:
   134	
   135	"You've been reading the strata. Old, older, oldest — I know, I read your face, you've dated these past anything the Compact admits." He tapped the board. "Here's what the strata can't tell you. These aren't the ruins of something. This is the *documentation* of something. Somebody wrote these down so the thing could be *built the same way twice*." He sat back. "In thirty years I never had the stone to match the text. You walked in with three floors." A beat. "I don't think you're going to like what you've found, Karis. I've had a piece of it for a decade and I've never once slept well since."

