   265	She did it without a pebble, on a leaf of her notebook, going from sheet to sheet of the Hall's twelve with the pencil held upright. She had forty-one kinds of sign, and had had them for the better part of a year; and she knew now, of the twelve that stood in one row, which kind bore and which was carried. It did not give her the rest. She said so, twice, to nobody.
   266	
   267	"I shall want every row," she said. "On every sheet. He will have to go through all fifty-two with that reed, and I shall have to lay each one and write it; and only then shall I be able to say how many kinds are white."
   268	
   269	"That's your week," said Brom.
   270	
   271	"It is a good deal of my week."
   272	
   273	The man came down within the hour, and it was done as she had said. He went along the Hall's table through the afternoon with the reed, and she laid, and wrote; and between the sheets he went back to the plank. The second of its rows stood on the Stair's ninth sheet, nine signs long, and the third on the Ring's sixth. He found each as a carpenter finds a joint in the dark, by going to where it ought to be. Each time the reed came back to the plank, and the pebbles went down along the table's edge in the order in which they had gone down on paper, white and black and red.
   274	
   275	The fourth row was the shortest, of seven signs, and it stood near the foot of the Stair's twenty-first sheet. When he had found it he straightened, and put both hands in the small of his back.
