   277	[[TIER-B: "Same make." — the plan quotes this line whole but does not pin its speaker; staged here as Cleon's, of the board and the three floors, after its fourth row is found; coordinator to supply protected text]]
   278	
   279	Cael kept the flame where it was wanted, and did not hear what Karis answered. He saw her glove come down upon the table's edge, flat, an inch from her own pencil, where she had been told that nobody was to lean; and the man beside her looked at the glove, and for once did not say so.
   280	
   281	He had been listening to another thing since noon. It had been said over his head, to a big man, as a rate is stated; and one piece of it had gone into him like a splinter. It was the piece about a man.
   282	
   283	He had come to that town with one question. He had written at a fire that he was about to hand it to a stranger, and had been afraid of it for two days, in a loft, with a small pale woman's roll across his knee. He need not have been. The stranger had told a gallery, without being asked, that he could not take it.
   284	
   285	It ought to have been a relief; and he knelt on the rock, and felt for the relief, and it was not there.
   286	
   287	He wanted it tried.
   288	
   289	He looked at that squarely, with his breath going long and the flame standing up. Two feet from his right hand there was a pair of sore red eyes that could see what held a thing up. They had found it in a cutting that nobody living had made, off a drawing, in the time that it takes to swallow. He had a place under his breastbone that five readings had come to and none had entered. He had been told, in so many words, that those eyes were for walls and seals and boards, and not for boys. He believed it. He wished, all the same, that the grey head would turn two feet to the left and look.
   290	
   291	He did not say so, since there was nobody to say it to. The man took no more note of him than of the iron arm that his own lamp hung from, and Cael had asked to be exactly that.
   292	
   293	He put the wish where he put a cord, and a hair, and a date. He would write it tonight, and he would write that he had not spoken it.
   294	
   295	"Left," said the rough voice.
   296	
   297	He was there already.
   298	
   299	When the bell went they roped the boxes, and Brom took his on the right shoulder again, and nobody said anything about it. On the long stair Cael counted the steps behind that box, twenty-seven, and the twenty-two after them in the open air; and the big man did not once shift it to the other side, since there was no other side to shift it to.
   300	
   301	The dray was gone from the yard when they crossed it. Its two logs lay by the dock, where somebody had thrown them, and the wheel had been found. Brom looked at the place in passing, as a man looks at a piece of work that he has finished and been paid for.
   302	
   303	He wrote it in the loft, under the day.
   304	
   305	*Brom: the left shoulder wrenched, under a dray in the registry's yard, with no Path opened. He is carrying on the right, in the old healer's knot, against Oryn's word at the fork. I did not tell him to put it down. I couldn't have made him. Owed at the mill, to two healers, by me as much as by him. Entered.*
