    51	The half-door was shut and barred at that hour, and the woman who leaned on it was asleep. He went out by the carriage-arch, past the painted door. Its lamp burned, and the book lay open on its stand within, with a pen on a chain, for whoever came late and had a name to give.
    52	
    53	It was a quiet district at night. He had expected that. A town which lets its labour in by the piece does not keep it up after dark; the benches were empty, and the slates had been wiped, and the only wheels that he heard were the watch's handcart, two streets off, going its round with a lantern. He let it go by. Then he went up to the ring road, and along under the wall, as far as the chandler's.
