     1	# Chapter 31 — One at a Time
     2	
     3	Karis sat up that night against the boxes, with the lantern turned low between her boots and her notebook open on her knee.
     4	
     5	She had not struck a point for it; Brom had lit the thing at the kitchen fire, with a spill, as he did every evening now, and it gave a brown uneven light that she would once have been ashamed to work by. She had got used to it in six nights, and it appeared that a person could get used to a great deal.
     6	
     7	A man had spoken to her at the bell.
     8	
     9	She did not go over what he had said. She had it by heart, every word, with the place where he had stopped to rub his eyes; and she had put the whole of it into the box where she kept things that might be so and could not yet be shown. It was a large thing for that box, and the lid would not quite shut on it.
    10	
    11	He had believed her, and that was what she could not get used to. She had walked into a cook-house with fifty-two sheets and a lifetime of being smiled at by better-placed people, and a stranger had cleared three tables and given her his cushion and told her that he knew the hand. She had not understood until now how light a pencil is in the fingers of a person whom nobody attends to. She might draw a line wrong on one evening and rub it out on the next, and no harm came of it to anybody.
    12	
    13	The pencil was not light tonight. A man had waited ten years with a board, and thirty with a lamp in one hand, and he was going to build upon whatever she said next.
    14	
    15	So she would say nothing next. She would read a number.
    16	
    17	She turned back a leaf, to where she had copied the plank's four rows that forenoon, squared to a hand's breadth, with the lamp held low upon the left.
    18	
    19	Twelve signs, and after them two rings and two pecks; nine signs, and a ring and four; twelve again; seven, and a ring and two. She had that much, and it was sound; a child could have had it. The cutter had counted his own work in figures, and his figures were of the plainest kind that there is.
    20	
    21	It was what stood after the figures that had kept her awake.
    22	
    23	There were six signs after the first twelve, and four after the nine. After the second twelve there were six again; and they were the same six, sign for sign, in the same order, as had followed the first; and after the seven there were five.
    24	
    25	She knew what she thought it was. She had known it on her heels in front of the board, with a girl asking, and had shut her notebook on it.
    26	
    27	A careful man writes a sum twice, once in figures and once in words, so that the one may be checked against the other, and a clerk at any counter did it still. If this cutter had done it, then those little groups were the names of his numbers; and if they were, each sign in them was not a thing that bore or hung. It was a sound.
    28	
    29	It was a handsome idea, and it explained why two twelves were followed by the same six signs. It did not explain it any better than three others that she could think of. The six might be a maker's mark, set on every full row. They might say *checked*.
    30	
    31	She had a sentence for occasions of this kind, and had said it over a cord in a frozen yard.
    32	
    33	A person who knows what she is hoping to find will find it. She was hoping, tonight, harder than she had hoped for anything since she was a girl. That was the very reason why she could not be the one to say what those signs were. Something else would have to say it, which did not care.
    34	
    35	She took a clean leaf, and headed it in her small upright hand.
    36	
    37	*What would have to be so. And how it could fail.*
    38	
    39	If the groups were number-words, they were words in some tongue, and she did not know the tongue; but there were not many that it could be. The stone of that archive was older than the town's first wall, and the oldest paper in it would be the nearest in age to whoever had cut the board. She had been told that the old town's first tally-books stood in the eastern gallery, on shelves that she had not yet seen. A tally-book wrote its numbers out in full, as every clerk's did.
    40	
    41	She would not trust her memory of how such a hand spelled. She had read perhaps a dozen leaves of that age in her life, at Ternhall, in copies, and a copy carries the copier's wishes. She would have the book itself.
    42	
    43	And she would not look at the board again until she had written down, from the book alone, what the board would have to show.
    44	
    45	That was the whole of the method, and she set it out in three lines. From the old hand's own spelling of *twelve*, and of *nine*, and of *seven*, she would write which letters in each were alike, and which letter of one word stood also in another; a pattern of same and different, and nothing more. She would fold that leaf; then she would carry it to the plank, and lay it beside the cutting, and see whether the signs repeated where the letters did.
    46	
    47	If they did not, in a single place, the idea was dead, and she would have killed it herself before it could be built on.
    48	
    49	Under the three lines she wrote the ways in which it could fail. There were four. She found a fifth, and wrote that as well; and somewhere behind the fifth, with the lantern guttering and Brom breathing like a felled tree, she understood that she had not been so nearly content since a store behind a chimney.
    50	
    51	She was not happy. That was another thing, and it had terror in it. This was only the old plain pleasure of having found a way to be proved wrong.
    52	
