   285	*Two hundred and ninetieth day; Treswick; the loft; a hundred and thirty-four. The cut, at dusk: six, hooded, two from the yard and four from the lane, with staves, one bow and one dark lantern, and no Path opened by any of them. Nobody dead, none taken, no faces. Karis: the left palm, on a step's edge; seven stitches, Brom's; no healer, since a healer here wants a name. Brom: both shoulders. Lira: the right shoulder, on a wall, and the arm did not take it. V.: a wall, on the eleventh day of thirty; the knee has gone again, and he cannot hold a bowl.*
   286	
   287	*Finding, S.'s: they cannot be written here either. They wanted one of us to open, and a light shown for it, and a box; they got a wall and a rock, and they left when the light failed.*
   288	
   289	*They know the loft, and they've said so.*
   290	
   291	*Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
   292	
   293	He ruled a line under that, and sat with the pencil over the leaf for some time. Then he wrote the rest, and it came all at once, as the largest things did.
   294	
   295	*Eleventh. Iron Wall, from Vastin, in an alley, while he held a line for us with nothing behind him but the decision to. It's not Brom's — Brom gets harder, this holds. Since my Kindling I've learned to give ground and disappear. Now I've got the opposite in me, from the man who spent forty years being the wall they put in front of people like me. He held it for us instead. I don't have a word for what that is. I wrote it down anyway.*
   296	
   297	Under it he put two lines more, since they were owed.
   298	
   299	*Not told. I can't tell the taking without telling the rest, and the rest is six people's. He asked for the wall to be entered, and I said that it was.*
   300	
   301	He turned the book round, and held it out across the gap between two pallets. Lira read it with her chin down, and she read the last two lines twice. She did not say anything at all. She shut the book over her thumb, and gave it back, and lay looking at the slates.
   302	
   303	The lantern was nearly out. By its last brown light he could see Karis, on her back, with a strip of a big man's shirt lying white upon her chest; and she was not asleep, for her lips were moving still.
   304	
   305	She had fourteen letters, and three days left of a week that a man below had called his own. There was a plank of black oak propped against a table in the dark, two hundred yards under her head, and a bundle on a shelf above it that nobody had untied.
   306	
   307	Cael lay down. He put the book under the box's becket, where an awl had mended it.
   308	
   309	*This far?* said the eleventh, of the dark.
   310	
   311	*No*, he said. He had a lamp to hold in the morning, and he found that he knew already which way it would be wanted.

