   414	
   415	- **Where the Line is at Treswick.** The river is the Line; the sill (one long stone, worn hollow, a socket at each end for a bar) lies under the outer gate's arch. The hedged fields east of the bridge are "ground that the town uses and does not own", worked to the foot of the moor, with a watch that rides as far as the fields go. **The road's hold is lifted at the first hedge, in one half only:** they may speak; nobody declares ("Out there a declaration draws a thing with teeth. In here it draws a clerk.").
   416	- **The road in.** Wayposts of dressed stone at nine, six and three miles, each with a figure and three short bars one above another. The six-mile post stands by a brook with a plank bridge. At three miles a carters' pull-in: a stone trough, a ring of blackened stones, a thorn hedge, a bank and ditch at the road's edge. The gate is shut at sunset and opened at first light.
   417	- **The bridge.** Five piers; one cart wide, with a bay over each pier; a dark stone with no beds in it, laid dry and cut to fit; the roadway worn in two grooves deeper than a wheel's rim. The pale mortared gate-tower is cut to lie against the bridge's end, course by course: the bridge was there first. Vastin has ridden over it four times.
   418	- **The outer gate.** A keeper in undyed grey on a high stool, with a frame of three wires and wooden beads (feet, wheels, hooves); he counts and asks nothing. A second keeper beside a strapped book on a sloped desk, who writes only those who come to sell ("A load on wheels is the town's business. A load on a back is the back's.").
   419	- **How the town sorts (the mechanism).** Three walls. **Iron walks through the second gate by right; Bronze through the third.** A stranger who comes by right shows a certificate, which is read aloud and written with the hour, and the entry goes to a counter before night. Everybody else goes up on a **card with a coloured edge** (blue for the second wall, buff for the third) that states a door, a purpose and a count; the keeper counts caps against the figure, makes one stroke, and tears a corner off the card. Cards carry an hour and a day. Keepers wear the gate's coat: grey, dark blue, pale buff. The second gate has two keepers and a groove for a portcullis; the third is a low round arch with one old keeper.
   420	- **The outer district.** The brokers' street from the gate to the bluff's foot, with a slate tally board at every door (*Wanted*; a load or a number of hands; the landing, the second wall or the third; a rate; a daub of colour for the card that goes with the work); the porters' bench; loads up on the right hand and down on the left. The rope-walk: a shed three hundred yards long against the inside of the town wall, ending in a yard of tar barrels above a quay; **Brom found four-strand laid round a heart, asked the price of sixty yards, found it fair, and did not buy** ("I'll take it going out"); no figure is printed. A public trough forty feet long at the street's head, where a brown mule that is not theirs was drinking (theirs has the white foot). A ring road under the second wall, right round the bluff. A landing with six flat boats, a treadwheel hoist and a cable ferry.
   421	- **The smith's two forges.** One with a hood, charcoal and a lad on the bellows, shoeing carters' beasts; one with no bellows and a white even bed held by a hired Ember in a dark blue coat (Karis: "Rank Two work, at the most"), shoeing a bay with a blue band in its forelock. Both smiths draw bar from one rack and nails from one keg.
   422	- **The inn with two doors.** A long house one street back from the trough, with a carriage-arch. A painted street door with a book on a stand inside it ("that side's for names"); a half-door under the arch where a woman takes money in front and writes nothing. **Two marks for the long loft over the stable for a week, the six of them, with the pot at dusk: 136.** A lower room off the yard with one long table and a hatch. Lira hung a horse-blanket over the loft's gable window, which looks up the hill.
   423	- **The road to the registry.** One street climbs from the trough through both gates and, eighty yards beyond the third, opens into the square. The registry's front door faces the station's steps across it. Loads go another way: from the third gate, a lane on the right hand under the wall, two hundred paces, with four alleys opening off it toward the square; then either the carts' street to the left (half a mile round the crown, past a bar and a weigh-beam where every load is written against an owner) or **the cut**: five flights, twenty-two steps, a stride and a half wide, between walls three men high with no door or window, down the bluff's north side to the registry's yard. (The cut's measures are BOOK_MAP §8's.) At the cut's head a waist-high parapet looks down over the whole town.
   424	- **The cook-house** built against the second wall beside the blue gate: a counter down one side, the wall's own stone behind it; a roofed wood-yard at its lower end where porters are fed through a hatch; the yard door open to the room.
   425	- **Cleon, as seen.** Not tall; grey hair cut short and badly; a coat the colour of dust; round shoulders; square pale hands with the nails pared to the quick; red-rimmed wet eyes, which he keeps shut while he eats; a voice "like a hinge". He looks at a sheet from its foot upward. He eats at the counter's end by the yard door at noon and goes down within the hour. **Cael knows him for Cleon at the counter** (the name said at a spring) and reads it once, written, at the head of the card in Lira's hands on the 283rd; nobody says it aloud. Karis told him at the counter what the floors are (the protected line: fused, grey, no join; three of them; fifty-two sheets), so his questions after it are of method only (rubbed or drawn; what light; which edge north; how far apart). **His terms (mine, one amended by the audit):** not on a counter; all of them, not a choice; they come down to him and he does not come up; his card, written at the counter (the matter; its bearer, one; hands, a figure), good for the next day only, at the second bell; no names given or wanted; not a word of what she thinks they are until he has looked; **one lamp goes down with them, his, held where he says, and no other carried light or fire of anybody's** (a light already burning below is not excluded). He pushed her sheet back without touching it. He left by the street door and stopped a full stride beyond it for three breaths. **He never looked toward the yard.** The Path is not shown; only that he rests his eyes.
   426	- **The provision, as Seln reports it.** The oldest and shortest leave in the registry's book: a practitioner registered under historical studies has standing entry to a lower archive, by the week, renewed at the yard door, and may have matter brought down to him. When matter is brought the form has a line for the matter (described), a line for **the matter's bearer, who is one**, and a line for hands. **Karis goes as the bearer**; nobody's name is on the card but the practitioner's. One clerk has kept the provision's book at the yard door for many years, with a single name in it. The exposure: the form neither requires nor forbids the clerk to ask who a bearer or a hand is.
   427	- **The quarry, as Seln reports it** (from a porter who has carried to the yard for eleven years and has never been below): the crown of the bluff was quarried from the inside, by galleries, before there was a wall on it; the later town kept its records there, since it was dry; the Compact built its registry across the quarry's mouth and never brought the old records up; one iron door in the undercroft, with the registry's wall built round its frame.
   428	- **The registry's back, the clerk and the door.** A sloping paved yard with a dock, sheds and a weigh-beam; five storeys of pale stone with small barred windows; **the lowest three courses are of the bridge's dark, dry-laid stone**. A low door, a hatch and a bench. **The clerk (re-ordered in repair):** no name; no particular age; a grey coat with ink on the right cuff; a narrow book bound in green cloth. He sits at the hatch with the book open and enters the matter before he looks at anybody; makes one mark in a narrow column; lifts his eyes along the paving at the height of a boot and writes *four* in a narrower one; blots it. Only then does he unlock the low door, stand on its sill and look at the people; his eyes rest on Vastin; his mouth opens and shuts; he looks at the card's old seal and at his shut book, and stands aside. He asked nothing and said nothing. The undercroft: a vault of pale stone with iron lamps, pillars, and the registry's lumber, with clerks' feet overhead; at a pillar the pale stone ends and the walls are dark and dry-laid and the air dry and cold. **The door:** low, broad, of black iron plates laid like slates, no lock or handle on the outer side, in a frame of dark stone to which the pale masonry is cut; the clerk knocks three times; it is drawn from within and swings inward; an iron sill worn bright; a stair going down into no light. Cleon counted the loads ("Three. And the case."), not the people.
   429	- **Vastin.** He told Cael beforehand that he would sit and could not rise without him; he sat on the bank at the three-mile post (the first time since the cut), in the inn's lower room (the second), and on the yard's bench (the third). **What he says of the file:** he read it first in the week before he came up the bluff, by the candles of four inns, and last on the morning he left a seal in a tray. **Why he declined (as repaired, in line with locked Book 6):** the reason he gave at Ostrand *was* the reason and he does not take it back: five young people had shown him what the office he served did with honest readings, and he would not lend it his name for one more; he wrote *declined* on a form that has no line for why, and would have written it had there been no night awake. **What he adds, and has told nobody else:** he had set out what a second reading could come to (a second silence, for which an office finds a word; or a plate that lit, which would have made his three days and nine thousand readings worth what a sheet of brass said); he could afford neither answer; it was the second that kept him awake; and when the pen was down he was relieved, on his own account, that he need never learn what the plate would say. "I had kept my hand off a door for a sound reason, and I was thankful of it for a poor one." He answers for the refusal and takes no credit for the gladness. His sentence from forty years of inquiries, "The instrument is not at fault. It has told you where its reach ends," is said aloud here; he wrote it once of a person, in an assessment that a seat declined to receive (Book 6). **His papers:** three sheets under one seal, in oilskin, which would open both gates and bring an officer down the hill; he does not know who reads the town's books at night. He has certified the town four times. In his window: every district he inspected had been told four days before that he was coming.
   430	- **Oryn's north road.** The first gate lies a day and a half north of the fork: a small hold with a wall of field-stone, a gate of oak bars, and a gate-house and bench on the wall's outer face; she has sat there on thirty rounds and never been through the gate. **The carter:** of Oxhollow, big, red-bearded, there for the hay; his right wrist was broken under a wheel a week after she passed Oxhollow before the snow, and strapped at Oxhollow's gate by the keeper's wife with a wet strap and two spoons; it knit a thumb's breadth out. She would not part it tired or without his word; he gave it in the morning; she re-set and knit it in an hour and was grey; six weeks in the binding; paid in oats. She spent her one day in hand there. **The second gate,** nine miles on, reached after dark on the 283rd: a boy of three whose chest she was to have heard a cold term ago, and who had mended himself; the keeper's son's wife in a gate-house built outside the wall, in labour since first light with the child lying across; "May I?" / "Yes. Yes. Do it."; she turned it from outside over some hours, giving it what she had kept for the road; **a daughter, alive, at first light on the 284th.** Nobody died. In her own head she has called the place in Cael "a stone" and "a join" and has decided that it was neither; she has no word.
   431	- **Small things.** Brom carries a flint, a steel and charred rag. Brom stood over Karis with a bowl on the 283rd. Oryn's empty bowl rides under the lashing of Brom's box. Children in the tar yard play at gates ("Then I'm the gate"). A house for the sick inside the second wall hangs its hours on a slate at the door: *First bell: buff. Second bell: blue. Cards, after the fourth, at the side door, as the house's means allow.* (Vastin: the ninth article of a schedule.)
   432	- **Roles (all unnamed):** two carters at the pull-in; the three gate keepers and the book-keeper at the outer gate; a broker's clerk; the ropemaker; the two smiths and the hired Ember; the inn's woman; an old woman porter at the kerb; a girl with eggs; the cook-house boy; Seln's porter; a yard-man; the clerk; Oryn's patients, the carter, the old woman who catches the hold's children, the mother and the keeper's son.
   433	
   434	### State at movement end (author; governed by the rulings above and by the surviving prose)
   435	*(Refreshed after repair r1, 2026-10-11. No fact of the calendar, bodies, money, loads or deployment changed in repair; the lines for Cael, Lira, Vastin, Cleon and the clerk are as the repaired text stands, and the markers are gone.)*
   436	
   437	**Calendar.** The forenoon of the **two hundred and eighty-fourth day past the Line**, under Treswick, on the head of the stair inside the iron door. Days on the page: 281 (the rise's foot; the posts; the three-mile pull-in), 282 (the bridge; the outer gate; the district; the inn; the district's edge; Seln's report), 283 (the cook-house at noon; the route; the evening), 284 (the inn yard at dawn; the second bell; two gates; the lane; the cut; the yard; the clerk; the door). Oryn: the first gate at dusk on the 282nd and the morning of the 283rd; the second gate from the night of the 283rd to first light on the 284th. No season, month or weekday name; no figure for the technician's date; no count of years since the Kindling.
   438	
   439	**Where everyone is.** Five inside the iron door: Karis, Brom, Lira, Vastin, Cael, with Cleon at the stair's head holding one lamp. The clerk is outside it with his lantern. **Seln is above ground, on no card, where he can see who comes to look at the door**; nobody saw him on the climb. Oryn and the brown mule are at the second of her five gates, north along the hill-foot, with three gates, the ford and Millrace ahead and her day in hand spent. Teague's five are east of the river (not on the page).
   440	
   441	**Cael.** Nineteen. **Ten fragments. Deployment thirty-two.** He has read himself a fourth and a fifth time (the 281st), round and not in. **Finding:** the reading feels what moves; the place has nothing that moves; the reading's nothing was never an answer; he needs an instrument for what does not move, and knows of two (the station, which he has promised to stay away from, and the man under the town). He has entered that he read Oryn's order for the leave he wanted, and that she is to be told so at the mill before anything else. **He has told Vastin** that the place was read twice that day and came back with nothing, and that what he reads with cannot read what does not move; **not what reads, or how.** Lira has read the log's entry. He has not told anybody the date, and has not written it. **He has not set foot on the square or the steps and has not looked at the crown**; the term has cost him something for the first time (the lane with four alleys). He has seen Cleon at five strides and was not looked at; he is afraid that the man will "look up". He wrote *Entered by count. That's the door.* in the log on the night of the 282nd, and said the #53 line that night as his own rough figure. He went into the registry as one of *four* in a green book, with his eyes on his boots. Body: sound; a box-strap's mark on his neck.
   442	
   443	**Lira.** Wind; Iron, Rank One. No burst. Arm: the nineteenth day out of the wool on the 284th; it carries a box by both beckets. **She has kept her eyes off the crown since the rise, unasked, hung a blanket over the loft's window, and walked between Cael and the square in the lane.** **Her registry paper is in her coat and has not been touched or shown.** At the cut's head she priced the whole town from the top ("a hill with a tax on it"; "Two gates. And a view."), shouldered her box, called the count ("Four hands," / Cael: "Four." / "Right.") and was first into the cut; Cael understood only afterward that it had not pulled. She has read the 281st's log and has said that she never asked for the question.
   444	
   445	**Brom.** Iron Skin; Copper. No grey. Right shoulder in the older healer's knot (it has not spoken). Right foot: five days left on the 284th; he favours it on hills. One box, with Oryn's bowl under its lashing. **The purse: a hundred and thirty-six.** Pike's thumbed slip in his shirt. He has looked at four-strand and will take it "going out". He lays seven bowls.
   446	
   447	**Karis.** Ember; Iron, Rank Three; shut since the fork. She has said, on the evidence of her own eyes, that the bridge is older than the walls; she has seen the same dark stone under the registry's back and under her bare hand in the undercroft, and has said nothing of it yet. She laid one sheet on a counter, answered a craftsman's questions, and **said that she came to ask how the thing stands, not what it says**. She has agreed to his terms and has kept her case to herself. She carried the card as its bearer; it has two corners gone. She holds the flat, the hide, the page in her coat, the ledger and her notebook. She has told Cael that she cannot tell her happiness from terror. She does not know what Cael meant by "look up" and did not ask.
   448	
   449	**Seln.** Shadow; Bronze. He lifted the road's silence and said what a town puts in its place. He learned the quarry, the provision, the clerk and the practitioner from a porter, without going above the trough; went up once on the 283rd as a counted hand with lamp oil and found the two ways round; **is on no card** ("I should cost you the count"). He has said the practitioner's Path and tier. The case is on him, shut; not mentioned.
   450	
   451	**Vastin.** No office; no age stated. **Right knee: the fifth day of thirty on the 284th**; on the stick; no kneeling, no running, no wall. It did nine miles on the 281st and twenty-two steps down on the 284th; the stick slipped once and he caught himself on the wall with the left hand. **He has sat three times.** He has told Cael the rest of why he declined the second reading: the Ostrand reason stands as the cause, and beside it his private relief at never learning what a plate would say (glad of a right act for a wrong reason). He holds what Cael gave him and has not asked what read it. He does not know the mechanism, what Cael carries, the sum, or what is under the bar; he does no date arithmetic. He has said once that he has papers he will not show. He consented to be the fourth before he was asked and went through three counts as a hand, looking at his boots, "very ill" and then a little better; the clerk looked at him after the line was blotted, opened his mouth, and did not ask. He has not asked to join and nobody has spoken of it. He carries the kit case, still against the letter of Oryn's instruction; Oryn does not know.
   452	
   453	**Oryn.** Tide; Iron, Rank Four, provisional. Two gates done of five. She parted and re-knit a carter's wrist at the first (asked; told yes in the morning) and turned a child at the second (asked; told yes). **Nobody has died under her hands.** She is spent to grey and a day behind her own reckoning. She has not been through a gate. She has the mule, her linen and the crossbow. She owes Cael the reading and has thought about it twice; she has heard of no leaf and knows nothing of what happened on the road's edge.
   454	
   455	**Cleon.** Named by the narration in chapter 24 (at the counter, and in the tags of the protected exchange) and once in chapter 26; never said aloud by anybody in this movement; Cael has read the name on the card. Rune Path, Bronze, by Seln's report only. He has set terms, written a card, and pulled the iron to. He has taken no notice of Cael. He has been told what the floors are (fused, grey, no join; three; fifty-two sheets) and nothing of what the sheets are thought to mean.
   456	
   457	**The clerk.** Unnamed. He asked nothing and spoke to nobody. *Four* is in his green book, blotted before he looked at a face.
   458	
   459	**Objects and money.** **136 marks** (138 less two at the inn's yard door). The reserve sealed in the third box, which is on Cael's back. Three boxes, the kit case, the flat and the hide are inside the iron door. Karis's fifty-two first copies are back in their box in their order. The card is in Cleon's hand. Still owed: sixty yards of four-strand to the Lowmarch ferryman. The loft is paid for a week from the 282nd.
   460	
   461	**Who knows what.** The mechanism: six, as before (Oryn among them); not Vastin, Cleon or the clerk. The inward finding of the 281st: Cael, Lira (from the log) and, in its outline, Vastin. The why of the declining: Cael and Vastin. The sum: Cael and Seln, each alone. Nobody has interpreted anything.
   462	
   463	**Open threads.** A date that two people know. A question that Cael has never carried to anybody, and a man who has not looked up. A dark stone under a bridge and under a registry. A clerk who nearly asked. A term in a log that now has a square at the end of four alleys. A paper in a coat that did not pull. A knee on its fifth day, and a healer who has not been told about the case. A reading owed at Millrace, and an admission owed with it. A rope to be bought going out.
   464	
   465	### Next-movement inheritance (Sol plan audit §8; also written into the next packet)
   466	Movement 5's packet carries the M3 baseline and its own 284th opening, but not the following surviving M4 particulars. Its author must inherit all of them:
   467	
   468	- **Who is below.** Inside the iron door are Cael, Brom, Lira, Vastin and Karis, with Cleon at the stair head; the unnamed clerk and his lantern remain outside. Seln is above ground, not on the 284th card or count, positioned to see who comes to the door. Oryn and the brown mule are at the second unnamed gate north (`ch25:87–117`; `ch26:183–279`; `AUTHOR-REPORT:101`).
   469	- **Loads and papers.** Boxes remain on Brom, Lira and Cael; the kit case remains on Vastin; Karis has the flat, hide/calfskin, copied page in oilcloth, dead surveyor's ledger and notebook. The fifty-two first copies are back in their box in order; Oryn's bowl is under Brom's lashing; Seln retains his closed case; Cleon has the two-cornered card (`ch24:199–225,275–293`; `ch25:89–117,229–237`; `ch26:195,213,261–267`; inherited M3 custody at `STATE_LEDGER:306–308`).
   470	- **What Cleon has seen and been told.** He has seen one drawn sheet from fused, grey, joinless floors; he knows there are three places and fifty-two sheets; he has been told they are older than what the Compact found, but not Karis's theory, purpose or proposed meaning. He has not noticed Cael. His terms are all sheets in order, brought below, no theory before he looks, his carried lamp under his control, no other carried light or fire (`ch24:65–181` after fixes 7–8).
   471	- **Inn.** The six—Cael, Brom, Lira, Karis, Seln and Vastin—have the warm long loft over the stable for a week from the 282nd, with the pot at dusk and seven bowls; the street door is for names and the yard half-door takes money without writing (`ch22:181–209`; `ch23:3–57,227–233`). Price paid: two marks. Purse: 136 (`ch22:195–199`; `ch24:265`).
   472	- **Count and restriction.** Karis is the unnamed bearer; Cael, Brom, Lira and Vastin are the four hands. Seln is not on the 284th count. Both upper keepers counted the card; the yard clerk asked nothing and wrote *four*. None of the four restricted names has been written, so the asset-restriction remains pending (`ch25:97–117,145–171,225–247`; `ch26:183–223`; `book-06 ch58:49–53`).
   473	- **Day and bodies.** It is the forenoon of the 284th. Vastin's right knee is day five of thirty, still stiff, with stick/no kneeling/no running/no wall; he has now sat three times and descended twenty-two steps, catching one slip with his left hand. Brom has five days left on the two right toes and the older shoulder knot; Lira's arm is day nineteen out of wool and has carried the box with both arms. Cael is sound apart from the box strap (`ch25:87,197–205`; `ch26:47–127,153`; `AUTHOR-REPORT:103,105,107,113`).
   474	- **Cael's state.** Ten fragments; deployment thirty-two. He has made the fourth and fifth self-readings, both around/not in, and concluded that Tide reports only current and cannot read the still place. He owes Oryn an admission at Millrace. Lira has read the log. The square/steps/crown promise still holds and has now cost him something (`ch20:95–215`; `ch21:49–65,227–247`; `ch26:9–25,175`).
   475	- **Vastin and Cael.** Vastin has confessed both the sound institutional reason for refusing certification and his private relief at not learning what another plate would say. Cael has given him the fact of two self-readings and the instrument's limit, but not what reads, fragments, mechanism, date or arithmetic. Each holds one fact from the other; the relationship is explicitly ground, not friendship (`ch21:121–223` after fix 4).
   476	- **Other live state.** Lira's Iron-first paper remains unshown; the ladder no longer pulls. Karis has recognized the bridge's dark stone under the registry and has not yet stated a conclusion. The sixty yards of four-strand remain owed and Brom intends to buy them going out. Oryn has delivered a live daughter, is spent and a day behind her circuit, has heard of no leaf, and still owes Cael the reading (`ch22:125–127,215–283`; `ch24:219–231`; `ch25:3–83`; `ch26:47–79,137–145`).
   477	
   478	**Movement 4 CLOSED (2026-10-11).** Same-author repair r1 (a bounded line repair; nothing re-staged): the nine coordinator-applied fixes kept and their joins smoothed (the boulder image made to agree with "*no length*"; Karis looks at three boxes and a case before "Four hands."; the cook-house questions after the restored exchange are of method only; "Four." given to Cael at the parapet); Vastin's refusal recomposed to locked Book 6 (the Ostrand case stated as the cause, twice, and not taken back; beside it "I had kept my hand off a door for a sound reason, and I was thankful of it for a poor one"; entered "on a form which has no line for why"); the two tracked passages in ch26 re-ordered (Lira prices the ladder aloud and shoulders the box before Cael understands; the clerk writes *four* and blots it before he looks at a face, then opens the door, looks at Vastin, opens his mouth and shuts it); Wind's ceiling removed; **Cleon's name comes to Cael at the cook-house counter and is then read on the card — nobody has said it aloud**; second tellings of the name-versus-count idea trimmed. Sol recheck: CLOSE WITH LINE FIXES; both tracked passages RESOLVED; every binding ruling borne out; the day arithmetic confirmed (161 days since the last self-reading; four days since the board; 282 days since the first sill; Oryn's gates). One punctuation fix applied by the coordinator (a broken dialogue join in ch21). The "New canon minted" and "State at movement end" sections above were refreshed from the post-repair author report. Final: 35,179 prose words; gates 0; overlap 0 unprotected (12 protected). Purse 136 marks; deployment thirty-two; ten fragments; the forenoon of day 284, inside the iron door under Treswick.

