22:4. **Where the Line is.** The river is the Line at Treswick, and the sill is under the outer gate's arch. The hedged fields east of the bridge are worked from the town and its watch rides them to the nine-mile post. Seln lifts the road's silence at the first hedge and says what replaces it: outside, a declaration draws what hunts; inside, it draws a clerk. Nobody declares, on either side.
28:10. **The inn with two doors (mine).** A street door with a book on a stand inside it, for those who lodge under a name; a yard door for carters and hands, who pay in front and are written nowhere. Two marks for the long loft over the stable for a week, with the pot at dusk: 138 → 136.
31:13. **Cleon's noon house** is a cook-house built against the foot of the second wall, with a counter inside and a hatch on its wood-yard. Karis goes to the counter alone. Cael stands in the yard with a bowl and hears it through the open yard door. Cleon never looks at him. The two names are kept out of one sentence.
37:- **Days:** 281 (the rise's foot; the nine-mile post; the six-mile post; the three-mile post at dusk) · 282 (the bridge; the outer gate; the district; the inn; the district's edge; Seln's report at night) · 283 (the cook-house at noon; the route found; the evening) · 284 (dawn in the inn yard; two gates; the lane; the cut; the yard; the clerk; the iron door). Oryn: the first gate at dusk on the 282nd; the second gate on the night of the 283rd to first light on the 284th.
38:- **Money:** 138 → 136 (two marks at the inn's yard door on the 282nd). Bowls at the cook-house hatch are coppers.
55:**Scene 1 — the foot of the rise; the first hedge; the nine-mile post.** Present: the six. Seln stops at the first hedge and lifts the silence, and says what a town puts in its place. The road fills: a dung cart, a boy with geese; nobody looks twice at people carrying boxes. At the post (a dressed stone with a figure cut in it) Lira sets her box down with her back to the town; Cael sees that she has not once lifted her chin toward the crown since the rise, and understands that she heard a term given at a fire and took it for her own. He does not thank her. *Changes:* the hold is off; Lira's discipline is on the page (packet: "Lira keeps the same discipline unasked"). *Fragments:* the read asks at the post what the stone weighs; told that it need not know.
57:**Scene 2 — nine miles to six.** Cael asks Karis what a historical-Path practitioner does. She gives what she has from a catalogue and no more: such a person reads how a made thing is built, and not what it says; she does not know which Path. He goes over what he is carrying toward a town with a station on its crown. He works out the line between the order and the reading (decision 2). He tells Lira, on the road, that he means to read himself at the next halt, and what he will and will not do; she says that he is to write down that she asked. *Changes:* Cael decides. *Protected:* Oryn's order, verbatim, recalled once: `Write it down and stop touching it.`
59:**Scene 3 — the six-mile post; the fourth reading.** Halt. Brom sees to his foot. Vastin stands. Cael lies down behind the hedge-bank with a palm on his breastbone and two fingers on his wrist. At ten the short reading gives him his own gait. Then the deep reading, and the ten named as currents, one at a time, with where each lives and what it costs to carry: Wind, the read, Ember, Pressure, Compression, Anchor, Blade, Shadow (known by what it takes away; shut), Storm, and the going-along itself. *Fragments:* Tide, on himself, the fourth time; not counted.
61:**Scene 4 — the reading round.** He lets the going-along bend where it bends and follows it the whole way round the place for the first time. He learns how far out the leaning begins, and that the way is worn. Then he sees what he has: every one of his facts is about what moves. The instrument has never told him that nothing is there. It has told him nothing. *Finding (packet):* the reading feels current; the still place has none; **"a cord on a stone"** (once, narration). Rising, his hand on the waypost: the same nothing under his palm (decision 3). He needs an instrument for what does not move, and he knows of two, and is afraid of both.
63:**Scene 5 — six miles toward three.** Lira asks what he found and is told that it will be in the log tonight, where she can read it. The town grows: he can count gate towers. Vastin, beside him, says that he saw a man go behind a hedge and come back the colour of paper, and that he does not ask. *Changes:* Cael has a finding that he has told nobody.
69:**Scene 1 — the three-mile post.** The gates shut at dusk and a bound knee has done nine miles. A carters' pull-in: trough, fire-ring, thorn hedge; two carters at a fire of their own who nod and ask nothing. Brom lays seven bowls. Vastin stands in the hedge gap. *Fragments:* Shadow lifts as the light goes; told no.
71:**Scene 2 — the fifth reading; the stick.** At the road's edge, on the ditch bank, before the light goes, Cael reads himself a fifth time, briefly, to see whether a finding made at noon will stand at dusk. It stands. A stick comes along the verge. Vastin looks at the bank for some time, and then sits: he turns his back to it, plants the stick, and lets himself down on his arms with the bound leg straight out. **The first time he has sat since the cut.** He says why he will not say this standing. *Fragments:* Tide, on himself, the fifth time; not counted.
73:**Scene 3 — the file.** Vastin: he read the file (decision 6): what was in it, who wrote it, and that he was the last and highest of the people who had laid an instrument on a boy. `[[TIER-B: "You were the instrument," … "I was the error."]]` Then, verbatim, Vastin: **"I declined to read you a second time. You know that. What you do not know is why."** The why (decision 7).
75:**Scene 4 — ground.** Cael gives him the instrument's nothing (decision 8). Vastin does not ask how the place was read; Cael sees him leave the next question lying, as he once did on a bluff, and knows now that it is chosen. It is not friendship. It is ground. Vastin says that he will need an arm to get up, and is given one.
77:**Scene 5 — the log.** By the fire. He sets the day at the head. **The log, verbatim** ("color" stands). Then a deployment line. Lira reads it, at his offering, and says one thing about what he has and has not let her hold. The walls by night; neither of them sits facing the crown.
84:**Scene 1 — three miles; the bridge.** Karis stops on the bank. The bridge is older than the walls: five piers of a dark stone laid dry; the outer wall is pale, mortared, and built up against the bridge's end without being bonded into it. She says so, and then cannot say anything else for a while. They cross among carts. Cael keeps his eyes on the parapet. *Fragments:* the read asks what the bridge carries; told that it need not know.
86:**Scene 2 — the outer gate.** A sill of stone under the arch, with sockets for a bar. Keepers in undyed grey; a frame of beads; a book on a sloped desk, strapped shut. The keeper's thumb moves six beads and he asks for nothing. Seln has said why: the book is for those who want to trade under a name. Vastin goes through a gate without anybody standing up. *Fragments:* Anchor asks at the sill; told no.
88:**Scene 3 — the district as a mechanism.** The brokers' street: a tally board at every door, chalked with what is wanted, by the load and by the hand, and the colour of pass that goes with it. The porters' bench. The rope-walk; Brom prices sixty yards of four-strand and does not buy. The public trough, and a brown mule at it that is not theirs.
90:**Scene 4 — the two forges; the inn.** One forge with a bellows and a man sweating at it; one with no bellows, a white bed, and a woman in a dark blue coat standing by with her hands in her apron. Brom, verbatim: **"They shoe by the tier."** Up the street, the second gate and its coats. Then the inn with two doors. Two marks at the yard door: 136. Seven bowls.
92:**Scene 5 — cutaway, Oryn (the first gate; dusk of the 282nd).** A walled hold under the hill-foot. A carter of Oxhollow's is on the bench with a wrist that was strapped at Oxhollow's gate in the cold term and has knit crooked. "May I?" She reads it. It will have to be parted where it has joined and set again, and she will not do that tonight or without his word. He gives it in the morning. She spends more on it than she has spent since the cut, and thinks of a Gold by a window who had what he wanted on the first day; and of a set of ribs that she has walked away from unread.
99:**Scene 1 — the loft.** Who may go through the second gate, and at what price. Lira has registry paper in her coat and does not take it out; nobody asks her to. Karis has hers. Seln says again what a written name costs four of them. Vastin, verbatim: **"I have papers I will not show. I want that said once, so that nobody asks twice."** Seln goes out, for some hours.
101:**Scene 2 — the district's edge.** Cael walks with the second wall on his left hand, so that the crown is behind his shoulder. He stands a quarter of an hour across from the second gate and watches it work: who walks through on a folded certificate, who is ticked on a pass, who is turned back for the hour. *Fragments:* Anchor asks at the gate; told no.
103:**Scene 3 — the landing; the street that goes up.** The rope-walk's far end; the landing; children playing at gates. Where the brokers' street begins to climb, a porter tells him the way to the registry: straight up, through both gates, and across the square to the door that faces the steps. **The road to the registry crosses the square.** He does not put a foot on that street. The promise has a shape for the first time.
105:**Scene 4 — cutaway, Vastin (the inn's lower room; the afternoon of the 282nd).** He has come in by a yard door for the first time in his life. He sits. Every district he ever saw had been swept for him the day before; this one did not know that he was coming: **the first honest district** (the packet's phrase, once). He watches a porter's pass ticked and a man turned away, and catches his hand going to a pen. What he has in his coat: papers that he will not show, and a receipt in a small woman's hand. What a boy gave him on a bank. He does not know what it was that was read, and he has not asked.
107:**Scene 5 — Seln's report (night).** The quarry: the old town stands on the roof of a worked-out quarry older than the town, and the registry is built over its mouth. The provision: a registered practitioner of historical studies has standing leave below, by the week; he may have matter brought to him. Of the practitioner, verbatim, Seln: **"Rune Path. Bronze."** The clerk who keeps the provision's book at the yard door. The exposure: a pen held over a line, and whether its owner asks. `[[TIER-B: "Four hands." / "I've two." "Entered by count. That's the door."]]` Lira and Brom say plainly what it changes. The way to the registry, and Cael's two sentences in a book; Seln will find another. `[[TIER-B: the S8 close — "Last time we walked into a place like this," … "We're the first ones who are both."]]`
110:Lira beat: the paper stays where it is. Vastin beats: the yard door; the pen. Seln beat: the report.
114:**Scene 1 — the morning.** Karis chooses nothing: she takes one whole copy, all fifty-two, in oilcloth. Seln's rule for the cook-house: one at the counter; one in the yard with a bowl, who is nobody. Brom's foot keeps him at the inn; Lira goes with Seln to walk the two ways round. Cael goes with Karis because he wishes to see the man, and says so.
116:**Scene 2 — the cook-house.** The counter; the hatch on the wood-yard; the open yard door. A man at the counter's end with his back to the room. Karis lays the first sheet beside his bowl. `[[TIER-B: "Off floors," / "Fifty-two sheets."]]` The talk, heard through the door: he asks how they were taken (rubbed or drawn; by what light; which edge was north; whether she squared them), and she answers with strata, and neither of them says what the things might mean.
118:**Scene 3 — his terms.** All of them, not a choice. Down to him; he does not read on a counter. His pass, written there: the matter, its bearer, hands, a number. No names given or wanted. Not a word of what she thinks they are until he has looked. One lamp below, and it is his. The day after, at the second bell. He puts down his coin and goes out by the street door, and **stops a step late**, a stride past the place where a man stops who has merely remembered something. He does not turn. He has not looked at the yard.
120:**Scene 4 — the afternoon.** Karis afterward. Brom puts a bowl in front of her and stands there. Seln and Lira come in: the two ways round; why the cut. Lira with the pass in her hand.
122:**Scene 5 — the evening of the 283rd.** The boxes re-roped. The log. Karis does not sleep. *Fragments:* Shadow at dusk; told no.
129:**Scene 1 — cutaway, Oryn (the second gate; the night of the 283rd to first light).** The gate with the child that she was to have heard a cold term ago: he is well, and she hears him anyway. A woman of the house is brought to bed that night and the child lies wrong. "May I?" between the pains. Two rivers in one bed. She turns the child from outside, slowly, with the Path easing what her hands ask. A daughter at first light, and a cry. She sits against a wall, spent, and what she thinks of is a body with ten rivers and one place where there is none, and what she owes it.
131:**Scene 2 — dawn in the inn yard.** Seln sets out the count: a bearer and four to carry. He is not to be on it, and says why. Three are plain. For the fourth, eyes go to a man on a stick; Vastin, verbatim: **"Yes"**, before anybody can ask. Loads.
133:**Scene 3 — the second gate.** Dark blue coats. The pass counted with a pen. Lira goes under a box through a gate that her own paper would have opened. A keeper looks a breath too long at an old man with the lightest load. *Fragments:* Anchor asks; told no.
135:**Scene 4 — the slate town.** Steeper. What Iron buys: cisterns, glass, a schoolroom, a house for the sick with a board at its door. The knee. Brom's foot.
137:**Scene 5 — the third gate.** Pale coats. Counted again. The keeper says where loads go: the right hand, under the wall. Cael turns right with his eyes on the paving.
142:## Chapter 26 — The Iron Door (day 284)
144:**Scene 1 — the lane under the wall.** The square is a brightness at the end of each cross-alley on his left. He does not look. Lira walks on that side of him, unasked.
146:**Scene 2 — the head of the cut.** The whole town below them, three walls and two gates. Lira looks at it from the top. It does not pull. She prices it. Then twenty-two steps down, in five flights, with a knee that will not bend; Cael goes two steps below.
148:**Scene 3 — the yard.** Carts; a weigh-beam; a bench. Karis at the hatch with the pass. On the bench he writes, **verbatim: "Lira looked at the whole ladder from the top of it and it didn't pull. She priced it. She's carrying a box."**
150:**Scene 4 — the clerk.** He is not named. He looks at the pass, and at four people as a man looks at four sacks, and he does not ask. He writes a figure. `[[TIER-B: "Four hands," / "Four." / "Right."]]`
152:**Scene 5 — the door.** Down under the registry. The stone changes. An iron door older than the walls. A man inside it, on the stair's head, who takes the pass and looks at the boxes. Cleon pulls the iron to. Narration, the packet's words, once: **"The tiers closed, and the dark opened."**
181:**Kept out:** any figure for the technician's date; any count of years since the Kindling; a possessive before "Path" in narration; the square, the steps and the crown under Cael's feet or eyes; Lira's paper shown; Cleon's Path shown; a name for the clerk, or a question from him; a new hold name; Vastin's age; a death under Oryn's hands; a fragment acquired; the still place touched.
193:- **Chapter 26.** On the cut's fourth flight the stick slips; Compression comes up into Cael's heels unasked and is told to wait; the load does not come. Not a use. The registry's lowest three courses, and the undercroft beyond the last pillar, are of the bridge's dark dry-laid stone. Cleon's name is on the page once, in the last scene, and nowhere in chapter 24.

