+
+Scene numbers follow the manuscript's `---` divisions; scene 1 is the material before the first divider.
+
+## 2. Tracked passage
+
+| chapter:line | Manuscript opening words | Source sentence followed | Kind |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| `chapter-27.md:101–107` | “It was a long page. Lira counted the lines by the finger …” | `books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-10.md:21` — “He read to the end of the line. Then to the end of the page.” | split |
+
+The plan requires Cleon to be reading already and to finish his page before attending to the arrivals. The manuscript also keeps the source's fuller local chain in the same order: the waiting group follows his progress to the foot of the page → he reaches the end → marks his place with a slip → closes or turns the leaf over → turns to the group. The manuscript spreads that chain across Lira's counting, Brom's silent lowering of the box and Karis's patience, but those additions do not change its inherited spine.
+
+To break it without changing the required event, let Cleon speak while still facing the desk and direct the unloading by sound; have him mark or close the leaf later, off the causal line. Another clean order is Karis identifies the three tables → the loads come off → only then does Cael notice that Cleon has quietly finished reading and is waiting for them.
+
+No other passage met the threshold. In particular, the gallery geography, unfiled beat, yard-door problem, truthful deflection and its cost, lamp trade, cart rescue and offer, board/counter method, unspoken wish, Seln roof walk, four lamp reflexes, Karis decipherment, alley positions and tactics, quiet acquisition minute, injuries, notice, withheld mechanism, faction limit and logs are all specified by the packet/map or the source-blind pre-prose event list. Their manuscript staging and sentence development are new outside the one local chain above and the excluded protected wording.
+
+## 3. Dense-scene guidance
+
+No scene is densely tracked. Chapter 27, scene 2 contains one short inherited sequence; the local reorder above is sufficient, and no scene-level reconstruction is warranted.

